# A value routed by range, which the engine has no single primitive for.
#
# A comparator answers one question: is this less than, equal to, or
# greater than the value on my last port. Three ranges therefore need
# two of them in succession, each handing what it did not claim to the
# next — a ladder, where every rung takes the values below its own
# threshold and passes the rest down.
#
#   feed ──> under_ten ──less──────────────────────> collect.0
#                      ├─equal───┐
#                      └─greater─┴─> under_twenty ──less──> collect.1
#                                                  ├─equal───┐
#                                                  └─greater─┴─> spare
#
# `feed` places a box that wraps at thirty, so of every thirty values
# ten are under ten, ten are under twenty, and ten are the rest.
#
# The last rung's own less-than exit is what carries the twenties, and
# its equal and greater exits are wired nowhere at all: nothing ever
# reaches thirty, because the counter wrapped before it could. An
# unwired exit discards, which is the ordinary case rather than an
# omission — it is what an unused comparator branch always does.

station feed cycle_thirty p entry
  out 0 - under_ten.0

station under_ten keep c
  in 1 = 10
  out 0 - collect.0
  out 1 - under_twenty.0
  out 2 - under_twenty.0

station under_twenty keep c
  in 1 = 20
  out 0 - collect.1
  out 1 - spare.0
  out 2 - spare.0

station spare keep c
  in 1 = 30
  out 0 - collect.2

station collect three_way p result
